<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Alamance</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16 May <a href="/years/1771">1771</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · <a href="/today-in-history/may-16">On this day: 16 May</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Alamance was fought on 16 May 1771 in North Carolina, where Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-tryon">William Tryon</a>'s militia defeated about 2,000 Regulators, backcountry farmers protesting against corrupt officials and excessive fees. The Regulators were poorly armed and led, and several were later hanged for treason. The battle ended the Regulator movement.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/regulator-movement">Regulator Movement</a> in colonial North Carolina sought reform of the Currency Act and an end to the corruption of local officials, along with secret ballots, land reform and open government. In September 1770 a group of Regulators attacked the superior court in Hillsborough and rioted through the town for several days. In January 1771 the assembly passed the Johnston Riot Act, which allowed force to be used to disperse assemblies, and the Regulators were declared to be in rebellion.</p><p>In the spring of 1771 Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-tryon">William Tryon</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-bern">New Bern</a> with 1,000 militia and 8 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>, funded with £6,000 from the merchant Samuel Cornell. He set out from Hillsborough on 11 May towards the Regulators' camp south of Great Alamance Creek, in what was then Orange County.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Through the night of 15 May the Regulators sent envoys to seek a peaceful settlement, but Tryon ignored them. Herman Husband, a Quaker who opposed violence, left before the fighting, and fewer than half of the Regulators present were armed. At about 11:00 on 16 May Tryon had the Sheriff of Orange County read a proclamation giving them one hour to lay down their arms and surrender their leaders. When the hour was up their reply was &quot;fire and be damned&quot;.</p><p>Tryon's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> opened with grapeshot, followed by volleys from the militia. By the second volley most of the Regulators had fled, and those who remained fired back from behind trees and rocks. After 30 minutes Tryon halted the artillery and sent the militia forward; they routed the rest, chased them for several miles and looted their camp.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Losses are disputed. Tryon reported nine dead and 61 wounded among the militia and 300 Regulator casualties. He took 13 prisoners and executed one, James Few, the day after the battle, then marched through the Regulator country burning homes and crops and making the people swear allegiance and give up their firearms. Of twelve prisoners tried at Hillsborough, six were pardoned and six were hanged on 19 June 1771.</p><p>Many Regulators and their families then moved west beyond North Carolina. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a> several prominent Regulators became Loyalists, while all of North Carolina's Continental Army generals had served with Tryon at Alamance. The site is preserved as Alamance Battleground State Historic Site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Alamance” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Governor William Tryon built an expensive palace at New Bern, the capital from the 1760s, and crushed the Regulator movement of backcountry farmers protesting corrupt officials at the <b>Battle of Alamance</b> in 1771.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-north-carolina">Province of North Carolina</a></li></ul></section>
